  "content": {
    "id": "p53-q13-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "13",
    "prompt": "[Auxiliary circle of a hyperbola]\nThe equation $(x+y)^2+(x-y)^2=a^2$ is an equation representing a circle.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p53-q13-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "By expanding then completing the square, find the radius of a circle in this form, in terms of $a$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$r=\\dfrac{|a|}{\\sqrt2}$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}(x+y)^2+(x-y)^2&=a^2\\\\x^2+2xy+y^2+x^2-2xy+y^2&=a^2\\\\2x^2+2y^2&=a^2\\\\(x-0)^2+(y-0)^2&=\\frac{a^2}{2}\\\\r&=\\sqrt{\\frac{a^2}{2}}\\\\&=\\frac{|a|}{\\sqrt2}\\end{align*}$$\nThe centre is already the origin, so no further completion of the square is needed.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 30
      },
      {
        "id": "p53-q13-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "What kind of curve is $(x+y)^2-(x-y)^2=a^2$? (hint: expand and simplify)",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "A hyperbola, $y=\\dfrac{a^2}{4x}$, for $a\\ne0$. When $a=0$, the equation represents the two coordinate axes.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}(x+y)^2-(x-y)^2&=a^2\\\\x^2+2xy+y^2-(x^2-2xy+y^2)&=a^2\\\\4xy&=a^2\\\\y&=\\frac{a^2}{4x}\\end{align*}$$\nFor $a\\ne0$, this is a rectangular hyperbola with the coordinate axes as asymptotes. For $a=0$, $xy=0$, giving $x=0$ or $y=0$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 20
      },
      {
        "id": "p53-q13-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": {
          "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
