        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 19
      },
      {
        "id": "p53-q13-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "Verify the intersection coordinates found in part c algebraically.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\left(\\dfrac a2,\\dfrac a2\\right)$ and $\\left(-\\dfrac a2,-\\dfrac a2\\right)$, with matching signs.",
          "worked": "Expanding the equations gives\n$$\\begin{align*}2x^2+2y^2&=a^2\\\\4xy&=a^2.\\end{align*}$$\nFor $a\\ne0$, substitute $y=\\dfrac{a^2}{4x}$ into the first equation:\n$$\\begin{align*}2x^2+2\\left(\\frac{a^2}{4x}\\right)^2&=a^2\\\\32x^4+2a^4&=16a^2x^2\\\\32x^4-16a^2x^2+2a^4&=0\\\\16x^4-8a^2x^2+a^4&=0\\\\(4x^2-a^2)^2&=0\\\\4x^2-a^2&=0\\\\x&=\\pm\\frac a2.\\end{align*}$$\nSubstitute each value into $4xy=a^2$:\n$$\\begin{align*}x=\\frac a2:\\quad y&=\\frac{a^2}{4(a/2)}=\\frac a2\\\\x=-\\frac a2:\\quad y&=\\frac{a^2}{4(-a/2)}=-\\frac a2.\\end{align*}$$\nHence the intersections are $\\left(\\dfrac a2,\\dfrac a2\\right)$ and $\\left(-\\dfrac a2,-\\dfrac a2\\right)$. When $a=0$, $2x^2+2y^2=0$ gives the sole intersection $(0,0)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 55
      }
    ]
  },
